From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: djvalic <djvalic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 11:49:15 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] touch: a finger drag scrolls the Settings pages again; easier
 page arrows

On the AD5X (resistive TSC2007 panel, 800x480) a drag on a Settings
sub-page did not scroll it, a drag started on the page arrows did, and
the arrows were hard to hit with a tap.

Drag. Patch 0019's stop_drag_chain_on_buttons(), run by
create_xml_hidden() over every overlay, cleared LV_OBJ_FLAG_SCROLL_CHAIN
on every clickable, non-scrollable widget whose name has no "_row" and is
not "row". The Settings rows are named row_* and their setting_group
cards group_*, so both lost the chain, and LVGL's search for the object
to scroll stops at the first widget without it (lv_indev_scroll.c,
lv_indev_find_scroll_obj): a drag on a row scrolled nothing. The arrows
are added later (PageScrollController::attach), so they kept the chain,
which is why a drag from them worked.

The walk now clears the chain on buttons only (lv_button and its
subclasses, ui_button included) and never on a list row or inside one:
a name "row", starting "row_" or containing "_row"
(is_list_row_name()). Cards, containers, labels, icons and overlay roots
keep the chain, as upstream. Buttons keep what 0019 gave them: a press
on one does not start a scroll, so the Z jog keys' hold-to-repeat does
not run on under a moving page. Sliders, arcs and rollers clear their
own chain in LVGL. Narrowed rather than dropped: dropping the call would
have changed every overlay button back to upstream at once, for a bug
that is only in the rows.

The top-level Settings list is built with app_layout (lv_xml_create),
not create_xml_hidden, and the walk never reached it; the tests drag it
from a row with the arrows on and it scrolls, before and after this
patch.

Arrows. ui_button turns press lock off, so one stray reading off a
52x70 px arrow lost the press (PRESS_LOST, no click), and a wobble of
the scroll limit (10 px) turned the tap into a scroll. Both arrows now
have press lock on and the scroll chain off (set in attach(), after
ui_button's own setup): a tap clicks the arrow it started on, a drag
from an arrow pages instead of scrolling, and the rows drag the list.
Each arrow has a 16 px ext_click_area (page_scroll_gutter.xml), half
the medium-tier gap of 32 px, so a tap in the gap hits the nearer
arrow. The area stops at the gutter's edges, as LVGL hit-tests a child
only inside its parent. The arrows keep their size: a wider gutter
would take the same width from every long page's content.

Tests in test_kiln_drag_scroll.cpp: a drag from a row scrolls the
top-level Settings list and the Printing page; a tap on a row clicks it
and scrolls nothing; an arrow tap with one reading 90 px off still
clicks and pages; a 12 px wobble on an arrow pages and starts no
scroll; every pixel of the gap hits an arrow and a tap there pages; a
held Z jog key in a scrolling column keeps the press and scrolls
nothing; and what the walk clears.

The tests' stand-in for printer_has_speaker is a helix::Subject
(init_int), as HelixScreen 1.2's scripts/check_lvgl_subject_api.py
asks.

Upstream: not applicable (Kiln OS specific)
